<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Pukará</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 200 CE (approx.) · capital Pukará</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first major political and ceremonial centre of the Lake Titicaca basin, whose stone sculpture and sunken courts foreshadowed the later power of Tiwanaku.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Pukará grew from about 500 BCE on the northern side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Titicaca</a> basin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puno-region">Puno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. A large terraced ceremonial complex was built against a red sandstone outcrop, with sunken rectangular courts at its summit. It became the dominant centre of the northern basin.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pukará is thought to have been a chiefdom or early regional polity whose leaders controlled herding of llamas and alpacas, raised-field farming and trade in obsidian and other goods. It is known for carved stone stelae and statues, including figures holding trophy heads, and for polychrome incised pottery with feline and human images. Its sunken courts and stone sculpture tradition influenced later highland architecture.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The site declined after about 200 CE, and the centre of power in the basin shifted south to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tiwanaku">Tiwanaku</a>, which absorbed elements of its art and ritual. The modern town of Pucará is known for its ceramic bulls, an unrelated colonial tradition.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pukara.jpg" alt="Pukará" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: bitacoreta · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pukara_de_Quitor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pukará</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Unknown local cults</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pucara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-150&amp;focus=pukara" aria-label="Pukará on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2676 2674 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pukará lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3130 3049h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2996 2930h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2996 2930h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Peru. <a href="/atlas?year=-150&amp;focus=pukara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
